    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "founder_facts",
      "label": "Facts under the portrait (one per line)",
      "default": "Born 1933\nFounded Shrujan, 1969\nRolex Laureate, 2006"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "founder_lead",
      "label": "Lead paragraph",
      "default": "Born in 1933, Chanda Shroff was 36 years old when she started Shrujan. The vision she gave it can be traced back to two loves of her childhood: making things with her hands, and being of use."
    },
    {
      "type": "richtext",
      "id": "founder_body",
      "label": "Her story",
      "default": "<p>Her love for crafts and the handmade, for the beautiful that could be made useful, began when she was four or five years old. She made clay vessels that her mother baked so she could cook rice in them. ‘So they were not mere toys,’ she wrote, ‘I could use them.’ Her mother, her first playmate, passed away when she was nine.</p><p>Her father, a freedom fighter who had worked closely with Gandhiji, became father and mother. He believed in education for girls, and that they should make themselves <em>pagbhar</em>: dependent only on themselves for their financial wellbeing. Years later, in a drought when food and hope were hard to find, Chanda Shroff would find a way to make the craftswomen of Kutch pagbhar.</p><p>Her ways were quiet and solution-oriented, and she chose the difficult and sometimes the dangerous path. In 1971, during the India–Pakistan war, she set out for Banni, close to the border. In 1974 she sought out the refugees of that war in the Jura camp near Loriya, and made it her lifelong mission to popularise the precision and perfection of their Soof embroidery.</p><p>Her care for the craftswomen and her passion to safeguard a living heritage led to visionary projects: the Design Centre on Wheels, Pride and Enterprise and, most ambitious of all, the Living and Learning Design Centre. In 2006 she became the first Indian Laureate of the Rolex Award for Enterprise, an honour that put Kutch and its crafts on the global map.</p><p>Kaki passed away in 2016, at the age of 83.</p>"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "legacy_title",
      "label": "Legacy heading",
      "default": "Her legacy"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "legacy",
      "label": "Legacy (one per line)",
      "info": "Numbered in order. Leave empty to hide the list.",
      "default": "Work in quiet, effective ways\nMake everyone your own\nBe mindful of each person’s dignity and self-respect\nCreate equal opportunities for all\nStay relevant and forward thinking\nFind excellence and joy in life and in work"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "founder_quote",
      "label": "Quote",
      "info": "Set on the maroon panel that closes the chapter. Leave empty to hide it.",
      "default": "There will always be problems; the fun is in finding a way out."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
