      "label": "Milestones (one per line)",
      "info": "Year | Title | Text | optional theme asset for a photograph. Separate the parts with a vertical bar.",
      "default": "1969 | Thirty saris for Dhaneti | Volunteering at the drought-relief kitchen in Dhaneti, Chanda Shroff asks the women to embroider their traditional motifs on 30 saris. They sell at an exhibition in Mumbai, and the money goes straight back to the 30 craftswomen of the Ahir and Meghwaad Gurjar communities: the beginning of India’s first social enterprise to work with embroidery craftswomen. | story-origin-4.webp\n1971 | Into Banni, in wartime | With India at war with Pakistan and parts of Kutch still in drought, Chanda Shroff and Ranjan Shroff set off for Banni, close to the border. The Mutva craftswomen they meet begin to earn from their embroidery, and embroidery becomes an economic lifeline for Banni in the drought years.\n1972–1979 | Soof in the Jura camp | Shrujan Arts and Crafts takes shape. In the refugee camp at Jura, set up after the war, Sonuben teaches Soof, the counted-thread embroidery of the Meghwaad Maaru and Sodha communities. By 1975, 300 women in 14 villages have joined the Shrujan family.\n1980–1983 | Stencils, London and a Trust | Stencils replace hand-drawn designs, letting Shrujan give work to 700 women in 32 villages. The first international exhibition opens in London in 1982, and in 1983 Shrujan Arts and Crafts becomes The Shrujan Trust.\n1984–1988 | Drought, once again | Kutch is ravaged by a severe drought. Shrujan runs a cattle camp, distributes food kits and rebuilds houses for the Kodi and Vaadha communities in Nirona.\n1990–1996 | A campus at Bhujodi | The Shrujan campus is built near Bhujodi village. The family grows to 1,800 women in over 75 hamlets and villages, Chanda Shroff makes Kutch her permanent home, and Shrujan exhibits in Nairobi.\n1997–2000 | The Design Centre on Wheels | 2,200 women in over 85 villages now invest in education and health care for their children, and in cattle. Shrujan launches the Design Centre on Wheels to inspire the craftswomen and the next generation to keep the craft growing.\n2001 | After the earthquake | On 26 January Kutch is devastated by an earthquake. The Bhujodi campus becomes a base for two years of relief, Chanda Shroff keeps embroidery work flowing, and the Shrujan family expands to 4,500 women. Master craftswomen Rajiben Naranbhai Shamaliya and Jassiben Rupabhai Meriya receive UNESCO’s Award of Excellence for Handicrafts.\n2002–2004 | 1,130 panels on the road | The Design Centre on Wheels is completed: 1,130 panels in 16 embroidery styles, made by 550 master craftswomen from 12 communities, travel from village to village by bus. Shrujan’s first shop opens in Mumbai, and it exhibits at San Juan Capistrano and Santa Fe in the USA.\n2005–2006 | Pride and Enterprise | Shrujan begins documenting every embroidery community and style in print, photographs, sound and film. In 2006 Chanda Shroff becomes the first Indian Laureate of the Rolex Award for Enterprise.\n2007–2015 | Building the LLDC | The Design Centre on Wheels makes 83 visits to 163 villages and reaches 22,000 visitors. In 2009 construction begins near Ajrakhpur on the Living and Learning Design Centre, and in 2010 Shrujan Creations is formed to carry the craftswomen’s work to market.\n2016 | The LLDC opens | The Living and Learning Design Centre opens with a five-day festival for 4,500 craftspeople and their families, and its first gallery, The Living Embroideries of Kutch. On 23 August 2016, Chanda Shroff passes away. | lldc-gallery-1.webp\n2017–2018 | Honoured at home and abroad | Shrujan takes part in Lakme Fashion Week, receives the International Crafts Award and, on 8 March 2018, the Nari Shakti Award. 257 craftswomen receive the Kaarigar Award, LLDC holds its first Folk Festival, and Shrujan exhibits in Osaka, Japan.\n2019–2020 | Fifty years | Shrujan turns fifty and helps form the Kutch Craft Collective. When the pandemic shuts everything in 2020, customers’ Count on Me vouchers keep every craftswoman in work, and the craftswomen are amused to see the staff learn to work from home, as they had since 1969."
